+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
+    """
+    Mirror the colored source components across the full green guide line, then connect each original-copy pair with the shortest clear orthogonal path.
+
+    Input structure:
+    - The background is black, with one complete green guide line filling either a whole row or a whole column.
+    - Exactly one half of the grid contains 2-3 small 4-connected colored components; each source color is distinct and is not black, green, or grey.
+    - Grey wall segments of length 1-3 may appear on both halves and act as blockers; the opposite half starts empty of colored source components.
+
+    Rule:
+    - Use the guide line orientation as the mirror axis: a horizontal guide mirrors top-to-bottom, and a vertical guide mirrors left-to-right.
+    - Copy every source component to its mirrored position with the same color, leaving grey walls fixed in place.
+    - Process source colors in row-major order of their original top-left pixels, and for each original/mirrored pair draw the shortest clear orthogonal connector with at most one bend.
+    - Connector cells may pass through green guide cells but may not cross grey walls, other colors, or previously drawn connector cells of other colors; when equal-length connector options exist, choose the one whose bend is lexicographically smallest by row then column.
+
+    Key insight:
+    - The green line does not connect anything by itself; it only tells you where to reflect the source half, and the reflected copies then become the targets for obstacle-aware routing.
